WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.837
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:03.837 --> 00:00:05.920
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: Hi, my
name is Maya Kaczorowski.

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:07.794
I'm a product manager
in security and privacy

00:00:07.794 --> 00:00:09.700
at Google focused on encryption.

00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:12.010
Today, we'll be talking
about encryption at rest,

00:00:12.010 --> 00:00:14.860
both how Google encrypts data
by default and the options

00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:17.380
that we give you to encrypt
data as a developer on GCP.

00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:20.290
Google uses several layers of
encryption to protect data.

00:00:20.290 --> 00:00:21.790
First, at the
hardware device layer,

00:00:21.790 --> 00:00:24.160
we encrypt hard disks
and solid state drives

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:26.080
with a device-level key.

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:28.790
Then we encrypt data at
the storage system layer.

00:00:28.790 --> 00:00:30.537
Let's dive a bit
deeper into that.

00:00:30.537 --> 00:00:32.995
To understand how storage layer
encryption at Google works,

00:00:32.995 --> 00:00:35.840
you need to understand how
Google stores your data.

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:38.200
For example, when you upload
a cat video to Google,

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:40.570
it's broken into subfile
chunks for storage

00:00:40.570 --> 00:00:42.580
and distributed across
Google storage systems.

00:00:42.580 --> 00:00:45.340
Each chunk of data is encrypted
with an individual encryption

00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:47.320
key so that no
two chunks of data

00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:49.420
will have the same encryption
key, even if they're

00:00:49.420 --> 00:00:51.790
part of the same Google
Cloud Storage object,

00:00:51.790 --> 00:00:55.450
owned by the same customer,
or stored on the same machine.

00:00:55.450 --> 00:00:58.180
This gives us super low-level
cryptographic isolation

00:00:58.180 --> 00:00:59.860
of data.

00:00:59.860 --> 00:01:03.160
Access control lists ensure
that each chunk can be decrypted

00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:05.530
only by authorized
Google services,

00:01:05.530 --> 00:01:07.690
using data encryption keys.

00:01:07.690 --> 00:01:10.240
These keys encrypt data chunks
and are stored with the data

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:11.800
chunks themselves.

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:14.860
And of course, we never store
unencrypted keys on disk.

00:01:14.860 --> 00:01:16.450
In fact, the data
encryption keys

00:01:16.450 --> 00:01:18.880
are themselves encrypted
with another key for storage,

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:20.535
called a key encryption key.

00:01:20.535 --> 00:01:22.660
This is commonly referred
to as envelope encryption

00:01:22.660 --> 00:01:25.284
because one key is
enveloping another.

00:01:25.284 --> 00:01:26.700
The key encryption
keys are stored

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:29.610
centrally in Google's internal
key management service.

00:01:29.610 --> 00:01:31.230
Using a central key
management service

00:01:31.230 --> 00:01:34.370
makes storing and encrypting
data at Google scale manageable

00:01:34.370 --> 00:01:36.450
and allows us to track
and control data access

00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:38.524
from a central point.

00:01:38.524 --> 00:01:40.190
Data stored in Google
Cloud is encrypted

00:01:40.190 --> 00:01:44.247
at the storage level, using
either AES-256 or AES-128.

00:01:44.247 --> 00:01:46.580
As a customer, you can use
our default Google encryption

00:01:46.580 --> 00:01:48.350
just by using Google Cloud.

00:01:48.350 --> 00:01:50.990
We already encrypt your data
stored at rest by default,

00:01:50.990 --> 00:01:53.060
without any action required
from you, using one

00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:54.620
or more encryption mechanisms.

00:01:54.620 --> 00:01:58.160
For additional options, we offer
a cloud key management service

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:00.110
that lets you
generate, use, rotate,

00:02:00.110 --> 00:02:02.489
and destroy the encryption
keys that you manage.

00:02:02.489 --> 00:02:04.280
You can also bring your
own encryption keys

00:02:04.280 --> 00:02:06.500
to the cloud with our
customer-supplied encryption

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:07.460
keys feature.

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:09.264
To learn more, read
our white paper,

00:02:09.264 --> 00:02:11.180
"Encryption at rest in
Google Cloud Platform".

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:13.930
[MUSIC PLAYING]

